analysis:
StartCommand.swift head 173-179 adds a read-only advisory step. It computes servedModels via advertisedModels(...) and prints GLMWiredLimitAdvice.startNotice to stderr. It writes nothing and does not affect registration, attestation or routing. StatusCommand.swift head 64, 102 and 172-175 pass snapshot.hardware and print a status notice derived from the daemon state file's lastModelLoadError. The output is local to the operator. The doctor action guide in GLMWiredLimitAdviceTests.swift lines 56-66 is static text suggesting sudo sysctl commands and a root LaunchDaemon plist at /Library/LaunchDaemons. The operator must run it; the provider runs no privileged step. This is not a TB-010-style helper surface, and no regression was identified in these CLI paths.

The material open question is RegistrationWeightHashTests.swift lines 28-52 and 54-73. These tests show a new registrationWeightHash(for:snapshotDir:verifiedWeightHashesFile:computeHash:) path. For managed GLM models it returns a recorded hash from verified-weights.json on restart without re-reading the weights. Per the test comment on line 44, any write to a hashed file invalidates the record, likely through file metadata.

This bears on A-004, T-012 and T-027, and on the T-041 verified weightHash binding for SSD cache scoping. If invalidation relies only on size and mtime, an operator (ADV-001) could substitute weights while preserving that metadata. The same applies if verified-weights.json is operator-writable. Either way, the provider would register a stale hash that no longer reflects on-disk bytes, weakening the weight-hash check.

The implementation (WeightHasher and the registrationWeightHash source, plus the location and permissions of the records file) is not in this batch. Integration must verify four things:
- which file attributes are compared (inode, size, mtime, ctime);
- whether the record is integrity-protected or lives in a protected location;
- whether ProviderLoop registration and cache weightHash binding use this cached value;
- whether the threat model T-012/T-027 needs an update for cached hashing.

No finding was raised because the source is not visible.
